content.text:
Every part is described in one file, **`spec/gbatek.htm`** (open it in a browser and use the contents links at the top). Read only the GBA, ARM and THUMB chapters. Skip everything about DS, DSi and 3DS, and the ARM9/ARM11-only opcodes, because the GBA's CPU doesn't have them.

| Part | What it does | Difficulty | gbatek section(s) |
|---|---|---|---|
| **CPU** | Reads the game's instructions one at a time and does them: add, compare, jump, load, store. Has 2 instruction sets, ARM (32-bit) and THUMB (16-bit). | **Hard.** Biggest job, everything depends on it. | ARM CPU Reference: Register Set, Flags & Condition Field, Exceptions, ARM Instruction Summary, all "ARM Opcodes:" pages except the ARM9/ARM11 ones, THUMB Instruction Summary, "THUMB Opcodes:" pages, Instruction Cycle Times |
| **Memory / bus** | The address map: address X means BIOS, RAM, video memory or cartridge. Every CPU read and write goes through it. | **Medium.** Simple idea, many details (mirroring, access waits). | GBA Memory Map, GBA I/O Map, GBA System Control (wait states), GBA Cartridge Header |
| **Graphics (PPU)** | Turns tiles, sprites, backgrounds and palettes into a 240×160 picture, one line at a time. | **Hard.** Many modes plus effects (scaling, blending, windows). Worth 60% of the score. | GBA LCD Video Controller: Dimensions and Timings, Display Control, BG Control/Scrolling/Rotation, VRAM pages, OBJ (sprite) pages, Color Palettes, Window, Mosaic, Color Special Effects |
| **Sound (APU)** | 4 retro tone/noise channels plus 2 channels that play recorded sound samples. Mixes them into stereo. | **Medium-hard.** 20% of the score. | GBA Sound Controller: Channels 1–4, Channel A and B (DMA Sound), Sound Control Registers |
| **Timers** | 4 counters that tick at set speeds. Games use them for music timing and delays. | **Easy-medium.** | GBA Timers |
| **DMA** | Fast copy machine. Moves memory blocks without the CPU, triggered at set moments (new screen line, sound buffer low). | **Medium.** Timing details are tricky. | GBA DMA Transfers |
| **Interrupts** | A doorbell. Hardware says "stop what you're doing and handle this now" (screen finished, timer done). | **Medium.** Small amount of code, but must be exact. | GBA Interrupt Control, ARM CPU Exceptions, LCD I/O Interrupts and Status |
| **Buttons** | Tells the game which buttons are held. | **Easy.** | GBA Keypad Input. Bit layout is also in `spec/ABI.md`. |
| **BIOS** | Startup code plus helper functions the games call (math, copying, decompression). | **Free.** Already given as `spec/gba_bios_stub.bin`. Your CPU just runs it. | BIOS Functions (background reading only) |

The order the score rewards is CPU, memory, then graphics with timing, then interrupts, DMA, timers, then sound.

## A pretend CPU in Rust

```rust
// A pretend CPU: 16 number boxes (registers), some memory, and a loop.
struct Cpu {
    r: [u32; 16],     // registers r0..r15; r15 = "which instruction next" (PC)
    mem: Vec<u8>,     // memory: just a big array of bytes
}

impl Cpu {
    fn step(&mut self) {
        let pc = self.r[15] as usize;
        let op = self.mem[pc];          // 1. fetch the next instruction
        let a = self.mem[pc + 1] as usize;
        let b = self.mem[pc + 2] as usize;
        self.r[15] += 3;                // move to the following instruction
        match op {                      // 2. decode, 3. execute
            0x01 => self.r[a] = b as u32,                              // LOAD  ra, number
            0x02 => self.r[a] = self.r[a].wrapping_add(self.r[b]),     // ADD   ra, rb
            0x03 => self.r[15] = b as u32,                             // JUMP  to address
            _ => panic!("unknown instruction {op:#x}"),
        }
    }
}

fn main() {
    // Program: r0 = 2; r1 = 3; r0 = r0 + r1
    let program = vec![0x01, 0, 2,  0x01, 1, 3,  0x02, 0, 1];
    let mut cpu = Cpu { r: [0; 16], mem: program };
    for _ in 0..3 { cpu.step(); }
    assert_eq!(cpu.r[0], 5);
    println!("r0 = {}", cpu.r[0]);
}
```

It's just an array, a loop and a `match`. A real GBA CPU works the same way, but it has about 40 instruction types instead of 3, condition flags, and 2 instruction sets. `emu_run_frame()` would call `step()` about 280,000 times, the number of CPU cycles in one frame.

I couldn't run this example because Rust isn't installed on this machine (`rustc: command not found`). The file is at `/tmp/claude-1000/-var-www-html-test-projects/005f9e94-f8e5-4ebe-a362-77fc748b7f0d/scratchpad/cpu.rs`. You can install Rust from rustup.rs, or use the repo's Docker container, which has it.

